pliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nPositives:\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) - automatic recording over lifetime\n- Standardization across all projects reduces compliance risk from inconsistent implementations\n- \"Immutable ledger\" provides audit trail integrity - critical for regulatory scrutiny and potential enforcement actions\n- Removing administrative burden from researchers means they can't accidentally skip logging steps - this is a compliance culture win\n- Universal interface ensures no high-risk system slips through without proper logging\n- The API hook approach in deployment pipelines means logging is baked into the system architecture, not bolted on after the fact\n\nConcerns:\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - does the proposal ensure logs are retained for the full lifecycle? An immutable ledger helps with integrity but I need to confirm retention period alignment.\n- The scope seems focused on Annex III 1(a) type data (timestamps, input matches, operator identities). What about general high-risk systems under Article 12(2)? The framework needs to be flexible enough for all high-risk categories.\n- \"Secure, immutable ledger\" - good for integrity but I need to consider: who has access? Can regulators access it? Is there a mechanism for the notified body or market surveillance authority to retrieve logs?\n- The proposal says \"high-risk projects\" which is correct scoping.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, well-aligned proposal from a compliance perspective. It directly addresses the letter and spirit of Article 12. The automation aspect removes human error as a compliance risk vector. Standardization across projects ensures no gaps. The immutable ledger provides auditability. The main gaps are around scope (does it cover ALL high-risk systems or just certain types?) and retention/access mechanics, but those are implementation details that can be addressed in the design phase.\n\nFrom institutional survival 