ughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" This is exactly what the regulation asks for.\n5. The \"flags missing compliance fields during code merges\" aspect means non-compliance is caught early (when it's cheap to fix) rather than at audit time (expensive).\n\nConcerns/risks:\n1. Building a custom documentation generator that maps Git commits and experiment logs to specific regulatory technical documentation fields could be non-trivial in engineering effort. The \"automated\" part might require significant initial setup — mapping which code changes affect which documentation sections, maintaining the schema of compliance fields, handling edge cases where multiple systems share components.\n2. However, this is a one-time (or amortized) investment that then pays dividends continuously across all projects. Compared to hiring dedicated compliance staff or having scientists spend 10-20% of their time on documentation, the ROI case is strong.\n3. The proposal says \"automated system\" but the actual complexity of mapping code changes to regulatory documentation fields could be higher than implied. Some fields (like risk management system documentation) may not map cleanly to code commits.\n4. There's a risk of over-engineering — building an elaborate system that tries to be too clever and ends up requiring its own maintenance overhead, which is counterproductive.\n\nOverall assessment from my vantage point: This is a strong proposal because it (a) leverages existing infrastructure rather than demanding new capital expenditure, (b) protects the most expensive resource in the lab — scientist time — from administrative drag, (c) makes compliance continuous and embedded in workflow rather than episodic and disruptive, and (d) directly serves Article 12's requirements. The main risk is implementation complexity, but even a partial version (e.g., just flagging missing fields at merge time) would deliver significant value.\n\nI'd rate this highly from my perspective. It's resource-efficient, uses existing infrastructure well, protects scientific capacity, and the investment case is clear. I'll approve it with a high promise score — maybe 82-85. The slight deduction is because \"automated system\" could balloon in scope, and some regulatory documentation fields (risk management processes, validation protocols) don't map cleanly to git commits, so the initial design work needs to be scoped carefully to avoid becoming an open-ended engineering project that competes with research funding.\n\nLet me settle on 84 — strong approval, high promise, but I want to flag that the scope of \"automated\" needs careful bounding.\n",
