utomatic recording of events\" requirement of Article 12(1).\n\n**My assessment as a Development Engineer:**\n- The core technical approach is sound and buildable with existing tools. No novel R&D required.\n- Prototyping speed would be high - this is essentially CI/CD + template rendering + API calls to MLflow.\n- Technical risk is low for the MVP, moderate for full coverage of all documentation requirements.\n- The \"replaces manual record-keeping\" framing is aspirational; in practice it will reduce but not eliminate manual work (narrative sections, risk assessments).\n- It's a practical, buildable solution that addresses a real pain point (outdated docs during audits).\n- From my vantage: this is a well-scoped engineering project. Not groundbreaking, but highly valuable and technically straightforward to prototype.\n\nI'd say this clears the bar for approval from a technical viability standpoint. It's not a moonshot - it's a standard software pipeline that can be prototyped quickly with high confidence of success. The promise score should reflect that while it's very doable, it's not revolutionary and has some scope limitations (doesn't fully solve Article 12 logging per se, doesn't fully automate all documentation requirements).\n\nI'll approve it with a solid but not maximal promise score - maybe 78-82 range. It's technically viable, fast to prototype, well-understood technology stack. The main caveat is that \"fully automatic\" is an overstatement and it addresses the documentation side more than the logging side of Article 12.\n",
