ed approach is exactly what a compliance officer would want — uniform, auditable, and not dependent on individual researcher diligence. The fact that it's \"mandated\" through API wrappers rather than left to voluntary implementation is important for institutional survival (if one project forgets or skips logging, the institution fails compliance).\n\nFor market reputation: Having a unified, well-documented logging framework demonstrates organizational maturity to regulators and market participants. It shows we're not just checking boxes per-project but have systemic controls in place.\n\nI think this clears my bar comfortably. It's a core compliance infrastructure investment that directly implements a mandatory regulatory requirement. The promise score should be high — maybe 85-90. It's not perfect (lacks specificity on Art 12(3) details, retention periods, authority access), but as a foundational framework it addresses the right problem in the right way. The gaps can be addressed in implementation without changing the fundamental approach.\n\nDecision: Approve\nPromise: 88 — Strongly aligned with mandatory requirements, reduces institutional compliance risk significantly, centralized approach is exactly what I'd advocate for. Docked slightly because it doesn't explicitly address retention periods, authority access mechanisms, or the specific Art 12(3) sub-requirements in detail, but those are implementation details within this framework rather than reasons to reject it.\n",
