nce.\" The actual scientific contribution is unspecified. It's more of an enabling infrastructure than a research advance. However, the *potential* for enabling frontier research on emergent behaviors in operational AI systems is real, especially given that Article 12 mandates this logging anyway — so you're getting some of it \"for free\" and extending it.\n\nI think this is borderline. The intellectual merit is moderate-to-good because:\n1. Operational telemetry for AI (vs training) is genuinely underexplored scientifically\n2. Provenance/lineage tracking has reproducibility implications\n3. Converting mandated infrastructure into research data is smart resource allocation\n4. But the specific scientific questions are vague, and the core contribution is engineering\n\nI'll lean toward approve but with a moderate promise score. It has genuine research potential from my vantage point, but it's more enabling than directly contributing to the knowledge frontier. The \"emergent model behaviors\" angle gives it some frontier appeal, but the proposal doesn't articulate specific research questions well enough to be exciting.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My role is about knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact. This proposal:\n- Has a clear mechanism (logging infrastructure)\n- Has a stated purpose that bridges compliance and science\n- The \"emergent model behaviors\" framing is at the frontier but underspecified\n- It's an enabler, not itself a research contribution\n\nI'll approve it because the potential for enabling frontier research on operational AI behavior is real, and the integration with mandated logging means incremental cost. But the promise score should reflect that it's more infrastructure than science — maybe 62-68 range.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 65. It clears my bar because it has genuine potential to advance understanding of operational AI behavior (frontier), the provenance/lineage angle has intellectual meri