ientific impact. It doesn't produce publishable results, it doesn't create tools others can use (it's an internal wrapper), it doesn't advance the field's understanding of AI systems. The data it captures (epoch parameters, dataset hashes) is standard MLOps practice that every serious lab already does or will do regardless.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: there IS a small research-adjacent value here. Having automatic provenance tracking could enable retrospective analysis of training runs - e.g., correlating specific hyperparameter choices with downstream performance issues, enabling reproducibility studies within the lab. But this is a stretch - the proposal as stated frames this purely as compliance automation (\"ensures technical documentation remains up to date without diverting researcher focus from core experimentation\"). The framing explicitly positions it as freeing researchers FROM administrative burden so they can do \"core experimentation\" - meaning it's seen as overhead removal, not as a research tool.\n\nAlso, the proposal says \"all high-risk models\" and logs \"every epoch's parameters\" which could be enormously voluminous data with little scientific value per entry. The intellectual design question of what to log and why is barely engaged with - it's just \"log everything to a ledger.\" A more scientifically interesting version would ask: what provenance information actually enables meaningful post-hoc analysis of model behavior? What granularity serves understanding vs. mere compliance?\n\nMy assessment: This is a necessary engineering/compliance task, but from my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), it doesn't clear the bar. It's infrastructure work that supports research indirectly by reducing administrative overhead, but it doesn't itself advance knowledge, has modest intellectual content, and no meaningful broader scientific impact. A researcher would not design their career around building a compute cluster wrapper for logging epoch parameters.\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise: I'd say maybe 15-20. There's a small sliver of research-enabling value (provenance data could support reproducibility studies), and the \"without diverting researcher focus from core experimentation\" angle means it indirectly protects research time. But the proposal itself, as conceived, is not research-advancing. I'll go with 15.\n",
