graphic proof of log integrity or zero-knowledge validation. What it requires is: logs exist, they capture specific events, and they're kept over the lifetime. A much simpler approach — tamper-evident append-only logs with standard cryptographic hashing (Merkle trees, etc.) — would satisfy any reasonable interpretation of \"record-keeping.\" ZKPs are massively over-engineered for what the regulation actually asks for.\n\n4. **Scope creep**: The proposal mentions \"without leaking sensitive training data\" — but Article 12 doesn't require you to prove anything about your logs to a third party in zero-knowledge fashion. It requires YOU (the provider) to maintain records and make technical documentation available. This is not an adversarial setting requiring ZKPs.\n\n5. **Resource commitment**: Approving this means committing scarce implementation resources to a research project with very uncertain timeline and unclear path to production integration. The \"positioning as leader in regulatory technology\" language suggests it's more of a prestige/academic play than a practical engineering solution.\n\nMy decision: This fails my bar for technical viability and prototyping speed. It's a fascinating academic idea but not something I can see being prototyped into a working system within any reasonable timeframe, and the problem it solves is far beyond what Article 12 actually requires. I'll reject it.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, this has some intellectual merit (maybe 20-30) because cryptographic log integrity is a real concern in practice (tamper detection), but ZKPs specifically for \"proving internal states\" is so far from practical that I'd give it maybe 15. It's not worthless — the underlying problem of log integrity matters — but the specific approach and framing make it impractical for my domain. I'll say 18.\n",
