sal says \"for every project's funding\" and mentions \"logging and documentation\" as distinct line items — this is a governance mechanism to ensure compliance is funded, not necessarily that every project gets the same amount of compliance work. The \"mandatory compliance cost assessment\" at grant phase suggests the percentage could be calibrated per project.\n- Risk: if the fixed percentage is set too low, it still won't cover actual needs; if too high, it may stifle R&D innovation and make the institution less competitive. But that's a calibration issue, not a structural flaw in the proposal.\n- This is fundamentally an internal governance/resource allocation mechanism. It doesn't directly implement any technical logging requirement, but it ensures the resources exist to implement them.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is a force multiplier for actual compliance implementation. Without guaranteed funding, even perfect technical solutions can't be built or maintained.\n- It reduces the risk of \"compliance debt\" accumulating — a scenario where logging capabilities degrade over time because no one funded their maintenance.\n- It creates accountability: if compliance isn't funded as a line item and a violation occurs, it's harder to blame resource constraints.\n- For market reputation: showing regulators and customers that compliance is structurally funded (not an afterthought) strengthens trust.\n\nThis is a solid governance proposal that directly supports Article 12 compliance by ensuring the financial infrastructure exists to build and maintain logging systems. It doesn't implement anything technical itself, but it removes a critical barrier to implementation. I'd approve this with a high promise score — it's a structural enabler rather than a direct implementation, so not a perfect 100, but very strong from my vantage point.\n\nI'll give it around 82-85. It's excellent governance thinking but the \"fixed percentage\" rigidity gives me slight pause