\" (plural) suggesting a platform approach rather than per-system implementation.\n- The risk is creating a bottleneck — all research teams depend on this two-person team for their compliance infrastructure. But from a prototyping speed perspective, having a standardized tool they can plug into is faster than each team building its own.\n- \"Fixed percentage of institutional overhead funding\" — this is a resource allocation mechanism. From my technical vantage point, I care that there's a committed, stable resource for the engineering work. A fixed percentage provides predictability.\n- The proposal is about organizational structure and resourcing rather than a specific technical solution. But it directly impacts whether technically viable solutions can actually be built at appropriate speed and quality.\n\nFrom my vantage point specifically (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):\n- This is a structural enabler for good engineering practice in compliance tooling. Without dedicated people, compliance logging gets implemented poorly (hacky, inconsistent across teams, low test coverage) or not at all until auditors demand it. That hurts both technical viability and prototyping speed.\n- The \"compliance as infrastructure\" model is technically sound — you build a reusable logging framework/SDK once, and research teams integrate with minimal overhead. This actually speeds up their prototyping because they don't have to solve the logging problem each time.\n- Two engineers is lean but potentially sufficient for building a solid SDK + documentation + maintenance. It's not over-scoped.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a practical, technically sound organizational proposal that directly serves my domain concerns (enabling quality engineering of compliance tooling and freeing research teams to prototype faster). I'd approve it with a reasonably high promise score — maybe 78-82. It's not a specific technical architecture so I can't give it the highest score