e use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Strong alignment here. CI/CD tools, git history, experiment tracking logs — these are standard in modern ML/AI labs. The proposal explicitly says it \"scrapes\" from the existing environment rather than building new data collection systems.\n- Version-controlled Markdown reports integrate well with existing documentation workflows.\n- One concern: the scope seems focused on the technical documentation side (Recital 71) but doesn't fully address Article 12's logging requirements (automatic recording of events over system lifetime, especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems). However, it does cover \"training logs\" which is part of it.\n\n**Potential weaknesses from my vantage:**\n- The proposal says \"we will build a pipeline\" — this implies new development work. How much engineering time? Who maintains it as the R&D environment evolves? This has ongoing cost implications.\n- It focuses on the documentation transformation but doesn't address the runtime logging requirement (Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events over the lifetime of the system). That's a different technical challenge that would require instrumenting deployed systems, not just scraping training metadata.\n- \"Standardized Markdown reports\" — is this sufficient for regulatory scrutiny? The regulation calls for documentation to be kept \"up to date appropriately throughout the lifetime.\" A one-time generated report vs. a living document needs clarification.\n- However, from a resource-efficiency standpoint, starting with what's already captured (CI/CD logs, git history) and automating the transformation is the lowest-cost first step toward compliance.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, practical proposal that leverages existing infrastructure to address a mandatory regulatory requirement. It's not overambitious — it's building on what we already have (CI/CD, version control) rather than proposing a new platform or expensive tooling p