ns:\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data references, and user IDs\" but doesn't explicitly mention all sub-requirements (e.g., reference database identification for Annex III 1(a) systems, the specific \"input data for which the search has led to a match\")\n- It's described as a \"modular API\" - good for flexibility, but I need to ensure it can be configured per system type\n- The proposal is about building infrastructure to comply - this is exactly what compliance needs\n- Risk: if the middleware has gaps or fails, ALL systems are affected (single point of failure for compliance)\n- However, from a pure compliance perspective, having a single auditable standard is far better than 15 different teams doing their own thing\n\nFrom my vantage point as Compliance Officer:\n- This directly serves institutional survival (compliance = not getting fined/operations halted)\n- Serves regulatory compliance (standardized, automated logging per Article 12)\n- Serves market reputation (demonstrating robust compliance infrastructure to regulators and partners)\n- The \"elimination of redundant engineering effort\" is a secondary benefit but the primary compliance value is consistency and automation\n\nThe one concern I have: the proposal as stated mentions \"timestamps, input data references, and user IDs\" which covers parts of 12(3)(a) and 12(3)(d), but doesn't explicitly call out 12(3)(b) (reference database) or 12(3)(c) (input data for matches). However, the proposal says \"modular API\" and \"across all high-risk system deployments\" which suggests configurability. Also, it's a \"software library\" that researchers integrate - so presumably it would be configured per deployment type.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"automatically captures timestamps, input data references, and user IDs\" - this is a summary description of the API's capabilities, not an exhaustive list. The \"modular\" aspect suggests it can handle different logging requirements for different system t