cle 14(5) reference**: Article 14(5) requires human oversight measures for high-risk AI systems. The logging of who performed verification connects back to this. The proposal correctly identifies this link.\n\n4. **Completeness concern**: Article 12(3) has FOUR minimum requirements (a through d). This proposal only addresses (d). It doesn't address:\n   - (a) recording period of each use\n   - (b) reference database against which input data was checked\n   - (c) input data for which search led to a match\n   \n   So this is a partial solution. However, the proposal is scoped specifically to one element, and it's reasonable that different teams handle different parts.\n\n5. **Practical compliance value**: Having an authenticated verification portal with automatic identity logging is genuinely useful for demonstrating compliance. It creates an audit trail that regulators could inspect. This supports market reputation (demonstrating robustness) and institutional survival (reducing regulatory risk).\n\n6. **Risk of non-compliance if NOT done**: If the lab has Annex III 1(a) systems, failing to log verifier identities is a direct violation of Article 12(3)(d). This could lead to enforcement action under Articles 99-101 (fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for non-compliant high-risk AI).\n\n7. **Proportionality**: A \"lightweight\" OAuth2-connected portal is proportionate — it's not an over-engineered solution, just a focused tool that captures the specific data point required. This is efficient use of resources.\n\n8. **One concern**: The proposal says it \"directly fulfills\" Article 12(3)(d). I should note that while it addresses identification, the broader logging framework (automatic recording over lifetime per Art 12(1)) needs to be integrated with this. A standalone portal might not integrate well with the system's overall logging architecture. But as a component, it's valid.\n\n**My assessment**: This is a targeted, proportionate proposal that addresses a s