        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate a single proposal (P1746) from my vantage point as Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**The Proposal (P1746):** \"Privacy-Preserving Audit Trails via Cryptography\" — implementing encrypted, verifiable logs using zero-knowledge proofs or secure multi-party computation to protect natural person identities while remaining accessible to regulators. Specifically addresses Article 12(3)(d) which requires identification of natural persons involved in verification.\n\n**My professional vantage point considerations:**\n\n1. **Resource-allocation efficiency:** This is a highly specialized cryptography project. Zero-knowledge proofs and secure multi-party computation are extremely complex systems to implement correctly. The engineering effort, expertise required (cryptography specialists), and testing/validation burden would be substantial. For an R&D lab whose primary mission is developing AI systems, diverting significant engineering resources into building a novel cryptographic infrastructure for logging seems like a major resource commitment relative to the actual requirement being met.\n\n2. **Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:** This proposal has a \"nice-to-have\" quality. The regulation requires logs that record identifiers of natural persons involved in verification (Article 12(3)(d)). A straightforward, well-governed access-controlled log with proper data protection measures (encryption at rest, access controls, retention policies) would satisfy the legal requirement without needing ZKPs or MPC. The proposal is solving a problem that may not exist at this scale — the tension between record-keeping and data protection can be managed through standard information security practices and GDPR-compliant data handling without resorting to cutting-edge cryptographic primitives.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** Does the lab have existing infrastructure for this? Almost certainly not — ZKPs and MPC require specialized tooling, expertise, and validation that most AI R&D labs don't have. Building this from scratch would be a significant new capability investment with unclear ROI relative to the actual regulatory obligation.\n\n4. **Is this over-engineering?** The proposal frames a standard access-control/data-protection problem as requiring advanced cryptographic primitives. In practice, Article 12(3)(d) requires that logs record \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification.\" This can be achieved with:\n   - Standard authentication and audit logging\n   - Role-based access control on log storage\n   - Encryption at rest (standard AES-256 or similar)\n   - Data minimization practices under GDPR\n   - Appropriate retention policies\n   \n   ZKPs or MPC would add enormous complexity for marginal additional privacy benefit when the data is already restricted to authorized parties. The regulatory requirement is that logs EXIST and are ACCESSIBLE to regulators — making them zero-knowledge would actually conflict with the \"accessible to regulators\" part unless you build a very specific verification protocol, adding more complexity.\n\n5. **Risk:** Implementing ZKPs or MPC incorrectly could introduce security vulnerabilities. These are complex systems where subtle implementation errors can be catastrophic. For an R&D lab focused on AI development, taking on this cryptographic risk is disproportionate to the regulatory obligation.\n\n**My assessment:** This proposal is scientifically interesting but represents a significant over-allocation of resources for what is fundamentally a standard information governance problem. The \"tension\" between record-keeping and data protection that it identifies is real in theory but manageable with conventional practices at most organizations' scale. Committing scarce implementation resources to building ZKP/MPC infrastructure for logging would be poor resource allocation when the same compliance outcome can be achieved with existing security engineering practices and standard access controls. It's a research curiosity masquerading as an operational necessity.\n\nHowever, I should note: it's not entirely without merit. If the lab operates at very high scale or handles particularly sensitive data, there could be a case for more advanced approaches. But even then, the resource commitment would need to be justified against simpler alternatives first.\n\n**Decision:** Reject. This is over-engineering relative to the regulatory requirement and represents poor use of scarce implementation resources when standard security practices can achieve compliance.\n\n**Promise score:** From my vantage point on resource efficiency — this might get a 15-20. It's not terrible thinking, it identifies a real tension, but the proposed solution is wildly disproportionate to the problem as framed by the regulation. I'll go with 15.\n",
