        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2298 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal against Article 12 (Record-keeping), specifically focusing on:\n- Recital 71's purpose statement about traceability, compliance verification, post-market monitoring\n- Article 12(1): automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime\n- Article 12(2): logging for identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation\n- Article 12(3): specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification):\n  - (a) recording period of each use (start/end date and time)\n  - (b) reference database against which input data checked\n  - (c) input data for which search led to a match\n  - (d) identification of natural persons involved in verification (Article 14(5))\n\nNow let me assess proposal P2298:\n\nThe proposal is a \"High-Risk Traceability Module for Biometric Systems\" — a specialized auditing layer for Annex III requirements regarding database references and natural person verification. It focuses on:\n- High-precision matching logs\n- A dedicated verification interface where human reviewers sign off on results\n- Linking their identity directly to corresponding system log entries\n- Concrete technical solution for heavily regulated high-risk project categories\n\nFrom a Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\nStrengths:\n1. Directly addresses Article 12(3) requirements — specifically (b) reference database, (c) input data matching, and (d) identification of natural persons in verification. This is precisely the most prescriptive part of the regulation with minimum logging requirements.\n2. The \"dedicated verification interface where human reviewers sign off on results, linking their identity directly