        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2937\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A unified, default-on logging library directly operationalises Article 12(1)–(2) across all project teams, eliminating the greatest compliance risk in a multi-team R&D setting: inconsistent or absent per-project log implementation that would fail an audit under Recital 71's traceability requirement. The pre-built risk-event triggers map sensibly to Article 12(2)(a)–(c), and institutionalising this as infrastructure rather than leaving it to individual labs materially reduces our exposure to enforcement (up to €15 M / 3 % turnover) and protects the institution's standing with notified bodies and regulators. I note, however, that the proposal does not yet explicitly enumerate the Article 12(3) minimums for Annex III 1(a) systems (start/end timestamps of each use, reference database, matched input data, identification of verifying natural persons), so those must be confirmed in the implementation spec before we can certify full conformity.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2937 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is about a \"Unified Compliance Logging Library\" - a standardized software library integrated into all high-risk AI prototypes to automate event recording with mandatory logging schemas, pre-built triggers for risk-event detection, etc.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Article 12(1) requires that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified library ensures this is baked in by default rather than being an afterthought per project.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities to enable recording of events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation. Pre-built triggers for risk-event detection directly address (a), (b), and (c).\n- A standardized library reduces the risk that individual project teams implement logging inconsistently or incompletely - a major compliance risk in multi-team R&D institutions.\n- \"Guarantees technical viability during regulatory audits\" speaks to institutional survival - if we can't demonstrate compliant logging, we face enforcement action, fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations), and reputational damage.\n- Recital 71 emphasizes traceability throughout the lifetime - a unified library with mandatory schemas supports this better than ad-hoc implementations.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"All high-risk AI prototypes\" - is the scope correctly defined? Not all prototypes will end up as high-risk systems, and not all high-risk systems fall under Annex III point 1(a) (which has additional minimum requirements in Article 12(3)). The proposal seems to conflate different tiers of obligation.\n- \"Mandatory logging schemas across different project teams\" - while standardization is good, overly rigid schemas could impede legitimate R&D iteration early on. However, from a compliance standpoint, I'd rather have the library in place and adjust schemas than not have them at all.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3) minimum requirements (period of use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons). It mentions \"mandatory logging schemas\" but doesn't specify whether those schemas cover the Annex III 1(a) specifics.\n- \"Pre-built triggers for risk-event detection\" - this is good in principle but the specific risk events would need to be calibrated per intended purpose (Article 12(2) says \"appropriate to the intended purpose\"). A one-size-fits-all trigger set might not work.\n\n**Overall assessment from compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong, structurally sound proposal that directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12. From my perspective:\n- It reduces the risk of non-compliance by making logging a default rather than an opt-in per project.\n- It supports institutional survival by ensuring we can demonstrate compliance to auditors and regulators.\n- It protects market reputation by showing proactive, systematic compliance posture.\n- The main gap is that it doesn't explicitly map to Article 12(3) minimums for Annex III 1(a) systems, but \"mandatory logging schemas\" could reasonably encompass this if properly specified during implementation.\n\nThis clears my bar. A unified, automated logging infrastructure is exactly what a compliance officer would want to see institutionalized. The risk of NOT having this - individual teams implementing their own ad-hoc logging or worse, not implementing it at all - is the greater threat to institutional survival and regulatory compliance.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. Maybe 82-85 range. It's strong but has some gaps (doesn't explicitly address Annex III 1(a) specifics, doesn't mention data retention/lifetime requirements from Recital 71, the \"pre-built triggers\" need to be purpose-calibrated per Article 12(2)). These are implementation details though, not deal-breakers.\n\nLet me settle on 83 - strong approval but noting that the specific mapping to all sub-articles and Annex III tiers needs to be confirmed during scoping.\n",
