erse AI architectures, the \"regardless of specific AI architecture\" claim might be optimistic — some architectures may resist clean API interception.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** The proposal says it will be \"integrated into our internal base libraries.\" This suggests leveraging existing code infrastructure rather than building a standalone system from scratch. That's good for resource efficiency. However, \"internal base libraries\" might not exist in all teams' workflows, so integration could still require per-team work.\n\n**Risks/Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- Upfront cost: Building and maintaining a unified middleware is non-trivial engineering work. For a lab with limited funding, this is a significant commitment before any ROI materializes.\n- Scope risk: \"All high-risk models\" across potentially diverse architectures (LLMs, vision models, tabular classifiers, etc.) could make the \"universal\" claim difficult to achieve without extensive per-model adaptation.\n- Maintenance burden: A shared middleware becomes a critical dependency. If it breaks or needs updating for regulatory changes, all downstream projects are affected. This creates a single point of failure and ongoing maintenance cost.\n- However, compared to each team building their own logging, the total engineering hours saved likely outweighs the middleware development cost over time (assuming 3+ active high-risk model projects).\n\n**Verdict:** From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is a strong proposal. It's essentially an infrastructure investment that amortizes compliance costs across multiple projects. The key value proposition — removing per-project logging implementation while maintaining prototyping speed — directly addresses the tension between scientific ambition (researchers want to build fast) and funding constraints (compliance work eats engineering hours). The integration into existing base libraries shows awareness of leveraging curr